## Vercel Speed Insights Installation and Configuration

Successfully installed and configured Vercel Speed Insights for the Next.js 14 application with App Router.

### Changes Made:

#### 1. Package Installation
- **Modified**: `package.json`
  - Added `@vercel/speed-insights` version ^1.3.1 to dependencies
- **Modified**: `package-lock.json`
  - Updated with new dependency and its sub-dependencies (607 packages added)

#### 2. Integration
- **Modified**: `app/layout.tsx`
  - Added import statement: `import { SpeedInsights } from "@vercel/speed-insights/next"`
  - Added `<SpeedInsights />` component inside the `<body>` tag, placed after the `<AuthProvider>` wrapper
  - This placement ensures Speed Insights is loaded on every page of the application

### Implementation Details:

The project uses Next.js 14.2.35 with App Router, so I followed the recommended approach for Next.js 13.5+:
- Used the `/next` import path for automatic routing detection
- Placed the component in the root layout for global coverage
- No additional configuration or props required (framework automatically handles routing)

### Verification:

- ✓ Linter passed without introducing any new errors
- ✓ Code follows existing project patterns and TypeScript conventions
- ✓ Lock files updated with new dependencies
- ✗ Build test encountered pre-existing Firebase API key configuration issues (unrelated to Speed Insights changes)

### Notes:

The Speed Insights component will automatically:
- Track Core Web Vitals (LCP, FID, CLS, FCP, TTFB, INP)
- Send performance data to Vercel Analytics dashboard
- Work seamlessly with App Router's automatic route detection
- Have minimal impact on bundle size and performance

The integration is production-ready and will start collecting metrics once deployed to Vercel.
